  "interpretation": {
    "prompt": "A polygenic score trained on the pooled multi-ancestry cohort reports the higher global R\u00b2. An ancestry-aware score is better calibrated in the smallest group. Which one is worse?",
    "whatChanged": "The pooled-cohort PRS is trained on everyone at once, so its weights converge to the largest group's. The ancestry-aware PRS estimates each ancestry group's weights from that group's data and pays for the extra parameters in global R\u00b2. What it buys is calibration: in the smallest group it is far better calibrated than the pooled score at the same shrinkage. Raise the shrinkage and the pooled score's calibration collapses first, while the ancestry-aware score still holds when the pooled one has shrunk to nothing.",
    "whyItMatters": "This is the multi-ancestry polygenic score result from the framing slides: the score trained on the pooled cohort wins on the headline metric for most groups and fails the group that made up a few percent of the training data. A single accuracy metric rewards fitting the largest group while under-serving smaller or heterogeneous ones, and it is built from pooled effect estimates like module 1's.",
    "facilitatorNote": "This is the African-ancestry exception on the multi-ancestry PRS slide. Read the two panels against each other at the starting shrinkage: the ancestry-aware PRS looks slightly worse on global R\u00b2 and is markedly better for the smallest group. Then push the shrinkage to the maximum: the pooled score's weights go to zero while the ancestry-aware score is still predicting (switch the score select to see each one's weight path; the ancestry-aware path is drawn for the Admixed American ancestry group). A score chosen on the headline metric alone is the one that fails the smallest group first. The weight blocks are a three-block stand-in for a genome-wide weight vector, not named loci; say so if asked."
  },
